S[3]=dict(lbl="How do Wrzesniewski and Dutton define job crafting?|What is task crafting?|What is relational crafting?|What is cognitive crafting?|Why do people craft?|What is job crafting not?|What can you now answer?",cards=[
Q("What is job crafting in the original perspective, and how does it differ from related ideas?",
 "There are two perspectives on job crafting. This step gives the first and oldest, from Wrzesniewski and Dutton (2001). It sees crafting as reshaping the job and its meaning, and it gives you three forms to recognize in a case.",
 ["The original definition","Three forms: task, relational and cognitive crafting","Why people craft: three needs","What job crafting is not: i-deals and other proactive behaviour"]),
CARD("As the physical and cognitive changes people make in the task or relational boundaries of their work, to fit the job and its meaning to their identity and values.",
 "<b>Physical changes</b> change the form, scope or number of tasks; <b>cognitive changes</b> change how you see the job. <b>Boundaries</b> are the edges of your job: which tasks are in it, and which people. <b>Work identity</b> is how you see yourself as a worker. <i>Our example:</i> a teacher who adds a coding club to her week because she sees herself as someone who opens doors for children.",
 "in this view, crafting is about fit with who you are, not only about efficiency.",
 "the first form."),
CARD("Changing the type, form or number of tasks, or how they are done.",
 "<i>The chapter's example:</i> a maintenance technician interviewed by Berg, Wrzesniewski and Dutton (2010) started helping newcomers learn the job; he was so good at it that he became formally responsible for training.",
 "task crafting changes what you do.",
 "the second form."),
CARD("Changing the amount and quality of contact with others.",
 "<i>The chapter's examples:</i> getting to know colleagues with shared interests, counselling new employees, joining joint events. It also includes putting positive relationships before difficult ones.",
 "relational crafting changes who you work with, and how.",
 "the third form."),
CARD("Changing how you think about your job.",
 "<i>The chapter's example:</i> a customer service representative said that technically the job is entering orders, \"but really I see it as providing our customers with an enjoyable experience\". It can also mean reminding yourself how important your work is, for yourself, for the people you serve or for the organization.",
 "cognitive crafting changes what the job means to you, without changing the job itself.",
 "why people do any of this."),
CARD("Out of three needs: control over their work, a positive sense of self, and connection with others.",
 UL([("1","<b>Control</b> over aspects of one's work, to avoid negative consequences such as alienation."),("2","A <b>positive sense of self</b>, expressed and confirmed by others."),("3","<b>Connection</b> with others, a basic human need.")]),
 "crafting answers needs the job leaves unmet. Petrou et al. (2012) add a fourth reason: to create conditions in which you can work healthy and motivated.",
 "where the boundaries of the concept lie."),
CARD("Not a negotiated deal, not participation in redesign, and not mainly about performance: crafting happens on the employee's own initiative, aimed at person–job fit.",
 "<b>I-deals</b> (idiosyncratic deals) are arrangements an employee <i>negotiates</i> with the employer. Employee participation in job redesign is also different. Other proactive behaviours aim mainly at performance; crafting aims primarily at <b>person–job fit</b> (how well the job matches your abilities and needs) and motivation. Lyons (2008) calls it \"spontaneous unsupervised changes in one's job scope\". And it is not automatically good or bad for an organization: its effects depend on the situation. <i>Our example:</i> negotiating a four-day week with HR is an i-deal, not crafting.",
 "the key test in a case: who makes the change happen, the employee alone?",
 "check what you can now answer."),
RECAP("Original view: crafting reshapes the task, relational and cognitive boundaries of a job, on the employee's own initiative, out of needs for control, self and connection.",
 [("Definition?","Physical and cognitive changes in task or relational boundaries, to fit identity and values."),("Task crafting?","What you do (the technician who trains newcomers)."),("Relational crafting?","Who you work with."),("Cognitive crafting?","How you see the job (\"an enjoyable experience\")."),("Why craft?","Control, positive self, connection."),("Not crafting?","I-deals, participation; crafting targets fit, on own initiative.")],
 ["Which three needs motivate crafting according to Wrzesniewski and Dutton?","Which statement is wrong? \"Negotiating a four-day week with HR is job crafting.\" (it is an i-deal)","Classify: an employee starts seeing her job as helping people (cognitive crafting)"])],
 guides=G("Read what Lisa plans, one move at a time.","First decide <b>who makes the change happen</b>: Lisa herself, a deal with her employer, or her manager.","If it is crafting, <b>tap the panel</b> it changes: what she does, who she works with, or how she sees it."),
 gguide=G("Read what the person changes on the card.","Classify it: <b>task</b>, <b>relational</b> or <b>cognitive</b> crafting, or <b>not crafting</b> (an i-deal or top-down redesign).","Read why, then go to the next person."))

S[4]=dict(lbl="Why did JD-R reframe job crafting?|How does JD-R define job crafting?|What does seeking resources mean?|What does seeking challenges mean?|What do reducing and optimizing demands mean?|Do the measures hold up?|How do the two perspectives compare?|What can you now answer?",cards=[
Q("How does JD-R theory define job crafting, and how is it measured?",
 "The original view was rich, but hard to see in everyday work. The JD-R view turns crafting into small, daily actions on your demands and resources: the job crafting of proposition 6 in reading 1, and the engine of the gain spiral.",
 ["Why JD-R reframed crafting","Its definition and four strategies","How it is measured, and how the two perspectives compare"]),
CARD("Because early measures did not capture crafting as an everyday behaviour: people reported on average only 1.49 crafting episodes in the past year (Lyons, 2008).",
 "Wrzesniewski and Dutton called crafting everyday behaviour, yet asking people about it gave very few episodes. So Demerouti, Bakker and colleagues framed it in JD-R terms, to capture the small daily changes.",
 "a concept you cannot measure in daily life is hard to study or train.",
 "the JD-R definition."),
CARD("\"The changes employees proactively make to balance their job demands and job resources with their personal abilities and needs\".",
 "It unfolds daily and targets the concrete demands and resources around you. <i>Our example:</i> on a hectic day, a consultant asks a senior colleague for advice and blocks an hour without email.",
 "JD-R crafting is about balancing your own demands and resources, day by day.",
 "the first strategy."),
CARD("Looking for feedback, advice or autonomy, either structural (developing yourself, more autonomy) or social (consulting colleagues, asking for feedback).",
 "Petrou et al. (2012) named the strategies; Tims et al. (2012) split seeking resources into <b>structural</b> resources (how the job is set up) and <b>social</b> resources (what comes from other people).",
 "seeking resources builds the resource pile of JD-R.",
 "the second strategy."),
CARD("Looking for interesting new tasks or more responsibility, to stay motivated and avoid boredom.",
 "It includes staying busy during the day and asking for more responsibility once your tasks are done. People who seek growth seek challenges to stay motivated and avoid boredom (Csikszentmihalyi &amp; Nakamura, 1989). <i>Our example:</i> an accountant volunteers to lead the new software rollout.",
 "seeking challenges keeps you motivated and growing; people in active jobs (high demands and high resources) tend to seek it, because it promotes mastery (Karasek &amp; Theorell, 1990).",
 "two ways of handling demands."),
CARD("Reducing makes work less demanding; optimizing makes work processes more efficient and removes obstacles.",
 "<b>Reducing demands</b>: making work less emotionally, mentally or physically demanding, or protecting your private life; this may protect health when demands are extreme. <b>Optimizing demands</b> was added later by Demerouti and Peeters (2018): a more constructive way to deal with demands. Optimizing is another <i>reduction-oriented</i> strategy, but a more constructive one; reducing demands resembles \"task avoidance\", a withdrawal-oriented way of coping (Parker &amp; Endler, 1996). Nobody proposes \"reducing resources\", because it does not seem to be a purposeful behaviour. <i>Our example:</i> skipping a hard meeting (reducing) versus turning a weekly report into a template (optimizing).",
 "an exam option saying reducing and optimizing are the same strategy is wrong.",
 "can all this be measured?"),
CARD("Yes: the four-factor structure was supported, crafting varies from day to day, and others can see it.",
 "A four-factor structure (increasing resources, seeking challenges, reducing demands, optimizing demands) was supported (Costantini et al., 2021). Optimizing demands improved pilots' performance in simulator training (Demerouti et al., 2019). <br><br>Crafting correlated positively with proactive personality and personal initiative and negatively with cynicism (Tims et al., 2012). Petrou's scale captures <i>daily</i> crafting, Tims' scale <i>general</i> crafting; the share of daily fluctuation ranged from 31% (seeking challenges) and 34% (seeking resources) to 78% (reducing demands). Self-rated crafting correlated with colleague-rated engagement, employability and performance, and with peer-rated crafting.",
 "crafting is a measurable, visible daily behaviour, especially reducing demands, which changes most from day to day.",
 "the two views side by side."),
CARD("Conceptually, Wrzesniewski and Dutton look at fitting the job and its meaning to your identity; JD-R looks at balancing daily demands and resources. Empirically they overlap partly, but cognitive crafting and reducing demands stand apart (Mäkikangas &amp; Schaufeli, 2021).",
 "The original view is measured with the Job Crafting Questionnaire (JCQ; Slemp &amp; Vella-Brodrick, 2013), which correlated positively with proactive behaviour and positive work functioning, and negatively with negative affect; the JD-R view with Tims et al.'s (2012) scale. Task and relational crafting overlap with seeking challenges and resources. <b>Empirically different</b> means that when both are measured in the same people, they do not collapse into one thing. <i>Our example:</i> \"I see my job as helping people\" (cognitive) has no JD-R strategy that matches it.",
 "know both views and where they overlap; an exam can ask for either.",
 "check what you can now answer."),
RECAP("JD-R crafting: daily, proactive changes to balance demands and resources, through seeking resources, seeking challenges, reducing demands and optimizing demands.",
 [("Why reframe?","Early measures found only 1.49 episodes a year (Lyons, 2008)."),("Definition?","Proactive changes to balance demands and resources with abilities and needs."),("Seeking resources?","Structural or social."),("Seeking challenges?","New tasks, responsibility."),("Reducing vs optimizing?","Less strenuous vs more efficient."),("Measures?","Four factors; daily fluctuation 31% to 78%; visible to others."),("Two views?","Empirically different; cognitive crafting and reducing stand apart.")],
 ["Why did JD-R researchers reframe job crafting?","Which statement is wrong? \"Reducing and optimizing demands are the same.\"","Code the diary entry: \"I asked my boss for feedback on my report\" (seeking social resources)"])],
 guides=G("Read Lisa's move, one at a time.","<b>Tap the part of the picture that changes</b>: the work itself, the obstacles, a new block on the demands, or a new block on the resources.","The tool gets its name in the toolbox. After six moves, do it again with Tom, the pilot."),
 gguide=G("Read the invented diary entry on the card.","<b>Code it</b>: seeking social or structural resources, seeking challenges, reducing or optimizing demands.","Read why, then go to the next entry."))

P1=("Who crafts overall?|Who reduces demands?|Which jobs invite crafting?|How do person and situation interact?|Do leaders and colleagues matter?|What can you now answer?",[
Q("Which people, jobs and social contexts predict job crafting?",
 "This is the left side of the chapter map: what leads to crafting. The two kinds of crafting, expanding the job and shrinking it, turn out to have opposite profiles, and that matters for the results in the next tab.",
 ["Which people craft, and which reduce demands","Which jobs invite crafting","How person and situation interact","The role of leaders and colleagues"]),
CARD("Overall crafting is higher in people with a proactive personality, openness, agreeableness, extraversion and self-efficacy; seeking resources and challenges is also higher with a promotion focus.",
 "This comes mainly from what the chapter calls perhaps the most extensive meta-analysis so far, by Rudolph et al. (2017); Lichtenthaler and Fischbach (2018) found largely similar results. A <b>proactive personality</b> is a tendency to take initiative; a <b>promotion focus</b> means being driven by gains and growth; <b>self-efficacy</b> is believing you can handle a task. Openness, agreeableness and extraversion are three of the Big Five personality traits, all positively related to seeking resources and challenges (only neuroticism relates negatively to seeking resources). Older employees and those with longer tenure seek fewer resources and challenges; women seek more resources than men.",
 "expansive crafting comes from people who look for opportunities.",
 "the opposite profile."),
CARD("People high in neuroticism and with a prevention focus; conscientious, open and proactive people reduce demands less.",
 "<b>Neuroticism</b> is a tendency to worry and feel negative; a <b>prevention focus</b> means being driven by avoiding losses and mistakes. Reducing demands shows almost the opposite pattern.",
 "reducing demands is more common in anxious, loss-avoiding people.",
 "what about the job?"),
CARD("Jobs with workload and autonomy together, jobs rich in resources, and times of impactful change.",
 "Crafting happens on days with high demands combined with high autonomy (Petrou et al., 2012). Jobs rich in resources (autonomy, feedback, development, support) boost seeking resources and challenges. Times of impactful organizational change also trigger crafting (Petrou et al., 2015). Autonomy goes with <i>less</i> reducing of demands, which suggests that autonomy stimulates motivation and goal-directed behaviour instead of withdrawal.",
 "an exam option saying employees with much autonomy reduce demands most is wrong.",
 "person and situation together."),
CARD("Communication during change works differently for different people: good communication boosts crafting for the promotion-focused, poor communication for the prevention-focused (Petrou et al., 2018).",
 "Good communication signals opportunities, which promotion-focused people respond to. Poor communication feeds fears, which drives prevention-focused people to craft. <i>Our example:</i> in a vague reorganization, the worried colleague starts reorganizing her tasks to feel safe.",
 "the same situation can trigger crafting in different people for different reasons.",
 "the social side."),
CARD("Yes: leaders' support predicts crafting more strongly than colleagues' support, and colleagues' own crafting is contagious.",
 "Empowering and transformational leadership and good leader–member exchange (LMX, the quality of the relationship with your boss) predicted seeking resources and challenges more strongly than coworker support (Wang et al., 2020). And colleagues' crafting predicted each other's crafting (Bakker et al., 2016; Demerouti &amp; Peeters, 2018).",
 "a leader who empowers people, and a team where others craft, both spread crafting.",
 "check what you can now answer."),
RECAP("Expansive crafting thrives in proactive, promotion-focused people with active, resourceful jobs and empowering leaders; reducing demands goes with anxious, prevention-focused people.",
 [("Who crafts overall?","Proactive, open, promotion-focused people (Rudolph et al., 2017)."),("Who reduces?","Neuroticism, prevention focus."),("Which jobs?","High demands plus autonomy; resource-rich jobs; change."),("Interaction?","Good communication → promotion-focused; poor → prevention-focused."),("Leaders and colleagues?","Leaders matter more; crafting is contagious.")],
 ["A company communicates poorly during a reorganization. Who crafts more? (prevention-focused employees)","Which statement is wrong? \"High autonomy leads to the most reducing of demands.\""])],
 G("<b>Flip a switch</b>: personality, the job, the leader or colleagues.","Watch the two meters: green for expanding the job, red for reducing demands. The finding behind each switch appears next to the picture.","Try the two missions: fill the green meter, then make the red one win. <b>↺ All switches off</b> to restart."))
P2=("What does expansive crafting bring?|What does reducing demands bring?|How does crafting work?|How does this link to the gain spiral?|What can you now answer?",[
Q("What are the effects of job crafting on well-being and performance, and through which mechanisms?",
 "This is the right side of the map: what crafting brings. Whether crafting pays off depends on the kind, which is the most important exam point of this chapter.",
 ["The effects of seeking resources and challenges","The effects of reducing demands","The two routes through which crafting works"]),
CARD("More engagement, satisfaction and positive affect, less burnout and strain, and better performance.",
 "<b>Well-being:</b> more work engagement, job satisfaction and positive affect (Rudolph et al., 2017; Lichtenthaler &amp; Fischbach, 2018), and less burnout (Tims et al., 2013), physical complaints, depression and job strain (Rudolph et al., 2017). <br><br><b>Performance</b> (for crafting overall): a literature review found crafting largely benefits <b>in-role performance</b> (your core tasks) and <b>extra-role performance</b> (going beyond, such as helping colleagues), whichever perspective is used (Lee &amp; Lee, 2018); overall crafting related to both self-rated and other-rated performance. Single studies add more meaning, better person–job fit, more voice and more creativity.",
 "expanding your job is good for you and for your work.",
 "the exception."),
CARD("Less engagement and satisfaction, more strain and exhaustion, and lower performance: \"rather detrimental or no effects\".",
 "Reducing demands goes with less engagement and satisfaction and more strain and exhaustion (Lichtenthaler &amp; Fischbach, 2018; Rudolph et al., 2017; Petrou et al., 2015). It negatively predicted in-role performance (Petrou et al., 2015) and extra-role performance (Demerouti et al., 2015). Shrinking your task and relational boundaries also negatively predicted in-role performance (Weseler &amp; Niessen, 2016). <i>Our example:</i> a student who copes with a heavy course by skipping the hard parts: less effort today, but less learning and more stress later.",
 "an exam option saying all forms of crafting improve engagement and performance is wrong.",
 "why expanding works."),
CARD("Through two routes: by changing the job itself, and by raising engagement.",
 UL([("1","<b>Through the job:</b> crafting changes demands, resources and person–job fit, which then affect well-being and performance (Tims et al., 2013; Demerouti et al., 2015)."),("2","<b>Through engagement:</b> seeking resources and challenges raises engagement, which raises performance (Lichtenthaler &amp; Fischbach, 2018).")])+"A <b>mechanism</b> is the route through which one thing leads to another; here engagement is a <b>mediator</b>.",
 "crafting works because it improves the job and because it energizes the person.",
 "the bigger picture."),
CARD("The chapter does not name it, but its findings fit JD-R's gain loop from reading 1: resources → engagement → crafting → more resources.",
 "The chapter shows the crafting → resources → engagement part for the expansive kinds; the loop itself comes from reading 1 (rule 6, the ladder).",
 "for advice: encourage expanding, constructive crafting, not withdrawal.",
 "check what you can now answer."),
RECAP("Expansive crafting raises well-being and performance through a better job and more engagement; reducing demands has rather detrimental or no effects.",
 [("Expansive crafting?","More engagement and satisfaction, less burnout, better performance."),("Reducing demands?","Less engagement, more exhaustion, lower performance."),("How?","Through the job (fit) and through engagement."),("Gain spiral?","Resources → engagement → crafting → more resources.")],
 ["Through which mechanism does seeking resources and challenges relate to performance in Lichtenthaler and Fischbach (2018)? (work engagement)","Which statement is wrong? \"All forms of crafting improve engagement.\""])],
 G("<b>Predict first</b>: what happens to Lisa a month after she shrinks her job?","Watch the red column fill in. Then <b>run the month again</b> with expansive crafting.","Compare the two columns and the strip at the bottom: the two routes. <b>↺ Start again</b> to retry."))
P3=("Why is crafting useful during change?|What did studies during change find?|Can reducing demands ever help?|What can you now answer?",[
Q("Why is job crafting useful when organizations change, and what does the evidence show?",
 "Organizations change all the time: reorganizations, mergers, new systems. This is where crafting earns its place, and where reducing demands gets one twist.",
 ["Three proactive actions that help during change","What studies found during reorganizations","When reducing demands can help"]),
CARD("Because the three proactive actions that help during change are all forms of job crafting, which gives employees a \"strategic advantage\".",
 UL([("1","Maximize the resources that help you cope."),("2","Keep the work pressure of the change at an optimal level."),("3","Seek challenges that turn the change into an engaging experience.")])+"These come from Avey et al. (2008). Research suggests crafting can help employees adapt the change to themselves, which makes them more adaptive (<b>adaptivity</b>: how well people adjust) and helps the change succeed.",
 "during change, crafting is a way to stay in control.",
 "the evidence."),
CARD("Seeking resources and challenges helped; reducing demands mostly went with less engagement.",
 "Crafting episodes were linked to readiness to change (Lyons, 2008). In a diary study among employees facing changes, seeking challenges went with more engagement and reducing demands with less (Petrou et al., 2012). <b>The study:</b> Petrou et al. (2018), a longitudinal study during the reorganization of a Dutch police department, so the effects were more enduring. <i>What they found:</i> seeking resources went with more engagement, seeking challenges with more adaptivity, and reducing demands with less engagement.",
 "the pattern of the previous tab holds during change too.",
 "a twist."),
CARD("Sometimes: in austerity-led change, reducing demands went with better adaptive performance for people who judged the changes favourably, and worse for the others (Demerouti et al., 2017).",
 "An <b>austerity</b>-led change is one driven by budget cuts. In the same paper, a crafting intervention increased reducing demands, positive affect and openness to change, and raised openness to change and adaptive performance through more positive affect: it helped \"to some extent\" under austerity. <i>In plain words:</i> in a harsh change, cutting back can help people who still see the change positively.",
 "an exam option saying reducing demands always harms during change is wrong.",
 "check what you can now answer."),
RECAP("During change, expansive crafting helps people adapt; reducing demands mostly hurts, but can help some people cope with harsh changes.",
 [("Why useful?","The three helpful actions during change are crafting: a strategic advantage."),("Evidence?","Police reorganization: seeking resources → engagement, challenges → adaptivity, reducing → less engagement."),("Reducing ever helps?","In austerity-led change, for those who judge the change favourably.")],
 ["Name the three proactive actions that help during change and why crafting fits them","Which statement is wrong? \"Reducing demands always harms during change.\""])],
 G("A reorganization hits. <b>Try each of Lisa's three responses</b> in the picture.","Watch the board fill in with what studies found for each response.","Then solve the twist: <b>predict</b> whether reducing demands can help in a harsh, austerity-led change. <b>↺ Start again</b> to retry."))
